# HTTP response headers for Codeberg Pages (git-pages, Netlify `_headers` format).
#
# git-pages denies custom headers by default:
#
#   AllowedCustomHeaders []string `toml:"allowed-custom-headers"
#                                  default:"[\"X-Clacks-Overhead\"]"`
#   — https://codeberg.org/git-pages/git-pages/src/branch/main/src/config.go
#
# A rule containing even ONE non-allowlisted header is discarded *whole*
# (`validateHeaderRule` -> `AddProblem` -> `continue`, src/headers.go), and only
# the first matching rule is applied per URL (`matchPathRules`) — extra `/*`
# blocks do not stack.
#
# ── SETTLED: the allowlist really is just X-Clacks-Overhead ─────────────────
# Measured 2026-08-05 by deploying each candidate on its own path, so that a
# refusal could only discard its own rule. Every one was refused:
#
#   /robots.txt   X-Frame-Options                 -> not served
#   /humans.txt   Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy    -> not served
#   /favicon.svg  Strict-Transport-Security       -> not served
#   /sw.js        Content-Security-Policy         -> not served
#
# Each probe path kept returning X-Clacks-Overhead from the `/*` rule below,
# which is the proof the probe rules were thrown away rather than merged.
#
# Consequences — do not re-litigate without re-measuring:
#   - XFO and CORP are unreachable on this host. `frame-ancestors` cannot
#     substitute: it is ignored in a <meta> CSP, header-only by spec.
#   - HSTS is unreachable. Codeberg terminates TLS and sets its own.
#   - Mozilla Observatory therefore tops out at C+ here (score 60 = 100 - 20
#     for HSTS - 20 for XFO). Only a different host changes that.
#
# What ships instead:
#   - X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff — already sent by git-pages itself.
#   - Referrer-Policy — via <meta name="referrer"> in BaseLayout.astro.
#   - The real CSP — an Astro-generated <meta> with per-build hashes.

/*
  X-Clacks-Overhead: GNU Terry Pratchett
